<!-- Introduction -->
<section>
    <h2>Overview</h2>

    <p>Managing z-index values across a large project quickly becomes chaotic. The common approach of using
        arbitrary
        numbers like <code>z-index: 9999</code> leads to an arms race where values keep increasing without any logic
        or
        system.</p>

    <p>unit.gl solves this with a <strong>semantic layering system</strong>. Instead of numbers, you use meaningful
        names that describe the <em>purpose</em> of each layer. The <code>z()</code> function maps these names to
        carefully structured z-index values.</p>

    <div class="code-block">
        <pre><code>@use "unit.gl" as *;

// Instead of this...
.modal { z-index: 9999; }
.header { z-index: 999; }
.dropdown { z-index: 100; }

// Use semantic names
.modal { z-index: z("modal"); } // 7000
.header { z-index: z("interface"); } // 4000
.dropdown { z-index: z("content"); } // 2000</code></pre>
    </div>
</section>
